{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "78",
    "case_summary": "A 10-month-old female infant was brought to the family doctor with symptoms of rhinitis, reduced feeding, and non-blanching spots on her thighs and cheeks. An upper respiratory tract infection had been noted in the week prior. There was no fever, diarrhea, or vomiting at the initial visit. The infant, the only child of non-consanguineous parents, had a normal term delivery with forceps and no significant medical history, although the parents had previously lost a child due to HELLP syndrome during pregnancy. Upon hospital admission, the infant was alert but pale with a body temperature of 36.2°C. Cardiovascular examination showed a heart rate of 160–170/min, capillary refill time of 2–3 seconds, blood pressure of 114/76 mmHg, and oxygen saturation of 99% in air. Heart sounds S1 and S2 were normal with no murmurs. The respiratory rate was 40/min. Central nervous system evaluation revealed a normal anterior fontanelle, no meningeal signs, good muscle tone and strength in all limbs, no focal deficits, and no cranial abnormalities. Examination showed multiple petechial spots on both thighs and a few on her cheeks, which had appeared since the GP visit an hour earlier. With slightly delayed capillary refill and mild tachycardia, the infant was treated with intravenous ceftriaxone and a fluid bolus of normal saline. A bolus of human albumin solution (HAS) was given on the advice of pediatric intensive care, with instructions to repeat if tachycardia persisted. Laboratory results showed a red blood cell hemoglobin level of 83 g/L, with normal red cell indices (MCV = 73; MCH = 26.8; MCHC = 363). White blood cell count was 22.4 × 10^9/L and neutrophil count was 12.1 × 10^9/L, both elevated. Platelet count was significantly low at 14 × 10^9/L. Blood metabolic indicators were pH = 7.323, pCO2 = 5.28, base excess = -5.0, bicarbonate = 20.0 mmol/L, and lactate = 1.3 mmol/L. Biochemical analysis showed normal plasma sodium (140 mmol/L), potassium (5.1 mmol/L), and creatinine (53 μmol/L), but elevated urea (16.2 mmol/L) and alkaline phosphatase (ALP = 2537 iu/L). C-reactive protein was slightly elevated at 8 mg/L.\n\nA subsequent blood film showing fragmented RBCs together with increased polychromasia and thrombocytopenia. A stool sample returned positive for E. coli O157.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
